 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_N2d7b7XT2gzcq6Mz",
   "name": "Integrating e^x & 1/x",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Integrating e^x & 1/x"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I integrate exponentials and 1/x?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **integrals** involving$e^{x}$ and$\\mathrm{ln}x$ are"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\text{integral} e^{ } x d x = e^{ } x + c$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`equation`"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "where $c$ is the **constant** of **integration**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- For the **linear** function$(ax+b)$, where$a$ and$b$ are constants,"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "** **$\\text{integral} e^{ } a x + b d x = \\frac{1}{ } a e^{ } a x + b + c$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`equation`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- It follows from the last result that"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`equation`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- which can be deduced using **Reverse Chain Rule**\n- With **ln,** it can be useful to write the constant of integration,$c$, as a logarithm\n\n  - using the laws of logarithms, the answer can be written as a single term\n  - `equation`where$k$ is a constant\n  - This is similar to the special case of **differentiating**$\\mathrm{ln}(ax+b)$ when$b=0$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A curve has the gradient function$f'(x)=\\frac{3}{3x+2}+e^{4-x}$.\n\nGiven the exact value of$f(1)$ is$\\mathrm{ln}10-e^{3}$ find an expression for$f(x)$.\n\n> *To find *$f ( x )$*, we need to integrate the expression for *$f ' ( x ).$*   *\n\n$f ( x ) = \\int \\frac{3}{3 x + 2} + e^{4 - x} d x$\n\n> *Rewrite as two separate integrals which can be found individually, and factor out any constants.  *\n\n$f ( x ) = 3 \\int \\frac{1}{3 x + 2} d x + \\int e^{4 - x} d x$\n\n*Use the two results:* $\\int \\frac{1}{a x + b} d x = \\frac{1}{a} \\ln \\vert a x + b \\vert + c$ $\\int e^{a x + b} d x = \\frac{ 1}{a} e^{a x + b} + c$\n\n$f ( x ) = 3 ( 1 \\frac{1}{3} \\ln \\vert 3 x + 2 \\vert ) + - 1 e^{4 - x} + c\nf ( x ) = \\ln \\vert 3 x + 2 \\vert - e^{4 - x} + c$\n\n> *The question states that *$f ( 1 ) = \\ln 10 - e^{3}$*, so substitute in *$x=1$* and equate to the given expression.  *\n\n$\\ln 10 - e^{3} = \\ln \\vert 3 ( 1 ) + 2 \\vert - e^{4 - 1} + c$\n\n> *Simplify and solve to find *$c.$*  *\n\n$\\mathrm{ln}10-e^{3}=\\mathrm{ln}5-e^{3}+c\\,\\mathrm{ln}10-\\mathrm{ln}5=c$\n\n> *Recall from laws of logarithms that *$\\mathrm{ln}a-\\mathrm{ln}b=\\mathrm{ln}\\frac{a}{b}.$*  *\n\n$c=\\mathrm{ln}\\frac{10}{5}=\\mathrm{ln}2$\n\n> *Rewrite the full expression for *$f ( x ).$*  *\n\n`equation`  \n**or by combining logs, this could be written as **$f ( x ) = \\ln ( 2 \\vert 3 x + 2 \\vert ) - e^{4 - x}$"
  }
 ]
